Shomehow any 128bit UUID that has the bottom 96 bits exactly the same as the `Bluetooth_Base_UUID` the logic breaks down.

## Bug Report

### Expected Behavior
When calling `USBSerial.read()` from the Particle Boron, it should return `-1` when there are no characters available.

### Observed Behavior
Instead of returning `-1` it returns `0`.

### Steps to Reproduce
System info:
* Firmware versions: 1.4.4, 1.5.0-rc.1
* Device: Particle Boron

Steps:
* Flash the test app to the Boron
* Connect to the Boron's USB serial port
* Open a Serial Monitor for reading and writing to the USB serial port
* Write a single character on the Serial Monitor (other than \<Enter\>)
* You should see the ASCII code for that character followed by a lot of zeros
* Type \<Enter\>
* You should see that the zeros have stopped

### Test App

```cpp
#include <Particle.h>

SYSTEM_THREAD(ENABLED);
SYSTEM_MODE(MANUAL);

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    Serial.print(Serial.read());
    Serial.print(", ");
    delay(100);
}
```
This reads the characters from the serial port and writes them as integers back to the same serial port.

### References

It seems like there is an issue with control of the RGB led, that depends on what order `brightness` and `color` are called in.  If brightness is called first, it works as expected, however if you call color and then brightness, the brightness is not adjusted.  For example:

```
void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    while (!Serial.available());
    RGB.control(true);
    RGB.color(0, 0, 0);
    RGB.brightness(0);
}

void loop() {
    RGB.color(255,255,255);
    Serial.println(0);
    RGB.brightness(0);
    delay(1000);
    RGB.brightness(63);
    Serial.println(63);
    delay(1000);
    RGB.brightness(127);
    Serial.println(127);
    delay(1000);
    RGB.brightness(191);
    Serial.println(191);
    delay(1000);
    Serial.println(255);
    RGB.brightness(255);
    delay(1000);
}
```

This code won't turn on the LED until the second loop, and then will stay at full brightness.  It only seems to be recognizing `RGB.brightness(255)` from the end of the loop, and `RGB.color(255,255,255)` from the beginning. See https://community.spark.io/t/rgb-brightness-doesnt-seem-to-be-working-solved/6303

